      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Terraform and Azure DevOps\nAzure DevOps plays two parts in a\nTerraform setup. It is a system\nthat Terraform manages, through\nthe provider\n`microsoft/azuredevops` ([provider\ndocs](https://registry.terraform.i\no/providers/microsoft/azuredevops/\nlatest/docs)). It is also the\nrunner: Azure Pipelines runs\n`terraform plan` and `apply` for\nevery other system, such as AWS,\nSnowflake and Aiven. This map\ncovers both parts. The [Terraform\nmap](../terraform.md) explains\nTerraform itself.\n\nThe unit is one Azure DevOps\norganization, which holds\nprojects. A project holds Git\nrepositories, pipelines and the\nresources that pipelines use. A\npipeline is a YAML file in a\nrepository plus a pipeline\ndefinition in Azure DevOps that\npoints to it. Pipelines reach\nother systems through these\nresources:\n\n- A service connection stores the\ncredentials for one outside\nsystem, such as an AWS account\n([Service Connections](#service-co\nnnections-service-connections)).\n- A variable group stores named\nvalues and secrets for many\npipelines.\n- An environment is a named\ntarget, such as `production`, that\ncan carry checks, such as\napprovals.\n- A secure file stores a file,\nsuch as a private key, for use in\na run.\n\nThree gates decide whether a run\nmay use such a resource ([Three\nGates on a Resource](#three-gates-\non-a-resource-three-gates-on-a-res\nource)). The most important gate,\nthe checks, lives outside the YAML\nfile ([Checks Live Outside the YAM\nL](#checks-live-outside-the-yaml-c\nhecks-live-outside-the-yaml)).\n\nWhen Azure Pipelines runs\nTerraform, three more ideas\nexplain what happens:\n\n- Each job starts on a fresh\nmachine. A plan file from one job\nis missing in the next job, unless\nthe pipeline publishes it ([Each\nJob Starts Fresh](#each-job-starts\n-fresh-each-job-starts-fresh)).\n- The pipeline task that runs\nTerraform decides which\ncredentials reach Terraform ([The\nTask Shapes the Run](#the-task-sha\npes-the-run-the-task-shapes-the-ru\nn)).\n- Azure Pipelines hides secrets in\nlogs, but only when they appear in\nfull ([Masking Is Best Effort](#ma\nsking-is-best-effort-masking-is-be\nst-effort)).\n\nNow compare dbt Cloud. A dbt Cloud\njob holds the commands and the\nschedule of a dbt run. An Azure\npipeline holds the commands and\nthe triggers of a Terraform run,\nand its environment checks play\nthe role of a release approval.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental\nModels explains the ideas above.\nReading Pipelines and\nConfigurations says where to look\nfirst. Pitfalls lists the failures\nthat experienced users plan\naround. Good and Bad Decisions\ncompares the main design choices.",
      "fontSize": 18,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 22.5,
      "containerId": "node-47aa38506338",
      "originalText": "Terraform and Azure DevOps\nAzure DevOps plays two parts in a Terraform setup. It is a system that Terraform manages, through the provider `microsoft/azuredevops` ([provider docs](https://registry.terraform.io/providers/microsoft/azuredevops/latest/docs)). It is also the runner: Azure Pipelines runs `terraform plan` and `apply` for every other system, such as AWS, Snowflake and Aiven. This map covers both parts. The [Terraform map](../terraform.md) explains Terraform itself.\n\nThe unit is one Azure DevOps organization, which holds projects. A project holds Git repositories, pipelines and the resources that pipelines use. A pipeline is a YAML file in a repository plus a pipeline definition in Azure DevOps that points to it. Pipelines reach other systems through these resources:\n\n- A service connection stores the credentials for one outside system, such as an AWS account ([Service Connections](#service-connections-service-connections)).\n- A variable group stores named values and secrets for many pipelines.\n- An environment is a named target, such as `production`, that can carry checks, such as approvals.\n- A secure file stores a file, such as a private key, for use in a run.\n\nThree gates decide whether a run may use such a resource ([Three Gates on a Resource](#three-gates-on-a-resource-three-gates-on-a-resource)). The most important gate, the checks, lives outside the YAML file ([Checks Live Outside the YAML](#checks-live-outside-the-yaml-checks-live-outside-the-yaml)).\n\nWhen Azure Pipelines runs Terraform, three more ideas explain what happens:\n\n- Each job starts on a fresh machine. A plan file from one job is missing in the next job, unless the pipeline publishes it ([Each Job Starts Fresh](#each-job-starts-fresh-each-job-starts-fresh)).\n- The pipeline task that runs Terraform decides which credentials reach Terraform ([The Task Shapes the Run](#the-task-shapes-the-run-the-task-shapes-the-run)).\n- Azure Pipelines hides secrets in logs, but only when they appear in full ([Masking Is Best Effort](#masking-is-best-effort-masking-is-best-effort)).\n\nNow compare dbt Cloud. A dbt Cloud job holds the commands and the schedule of a dbt run. An Azure pipeline holds the commands and the triggers of a Terraform run, and its environment checks play the role of a release approval.\n\nThe map has four parts. Mental Models explains the ideas above. Reading Pipelines and Configurations says where to look first. Pitfalls lists the failures that experienced users plan around. Good and Bad Decisions compares the main design choices.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "terraform-azure-devops"
      }
    },
