      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 2418703937,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "text-1bff65d7b6b4",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1332.0,
      "y": 1936.5,
      "strokeColor": "#1E3A8A",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 394.8,
      "height": 560.0,
      "seed": 74571,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Reading a Terraform Pipeline\nRead the pipeline YAML of a Terraform root\nmodule in this order:\n\n1. Find the variable or parameter that names\nthe working directory. It binds the pipeline\nto its root module.\n2. Read `trigger` and its `paths`, then the\nbranch policy of the main branch. Pull request\nruns come only from the policy.\n3. Follow each `template:` reference. The\nsteps can live in shared template files.\n4. Find the condition of the apply step: the\nbranch, `Build.Reason` and any override\nparameter. It tells you what can reach\nproduction.\n5. Look for `deployment:` jobs with an\n`environment:`, then read that environment's\nchecks in the web interface. They are the real\ngate.\n6. List the service connection inputs of the\nTerraform tasks. For each connection, read its\nrole, its pipeline permissions and its checks.\n7. Read how the run selects its state: the\nworkspace, `-backend-config` values and\n`-var-file` values.\n8. List the secret sources: variable groups,\n`$(variable)` macros, `TF_VAR_*` environment\nvariables and secure files.\n9. Check whether the apply uses the saved plan\nfile, and whether the Terraform version is\npinned.",
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "baseline": 17.5,
      "containerId": "node-1bff65d7b6b4",
      "originalText": "Reading a Terraform Pipeline\nRead the pipeline YAML of a Terraform root module in this order:\n\n1. Find the variable or parameter that names the working directory. It binds the pipeline to its root module.\n2. Read `trigger` and its `paths`, then the branch policy of the main branch. Pull request runs come only from the policy.\n3. Follow each `template:` reference. The steps can live in shared template files.\n4. Find the condition of the apply step: the branch, `Build.Reason` and any override parameter. It tells you what can reach production.\n5. Look for `deployment:` jobs with an `environment:`, then read that environment's checks in the web interface. They are the real gate.\n6. List the service connection inputs of the Terraform tasks. For each connection, read its role, its pipeline permissions and its checks.\n7. Read how the run selects its state: the workspace, `-backend-config` values and `-var-file` values.\n8. List the secret sources: variable groups, `$(variable)` macros, `TF_VAR_*` environment variables and secure files.\n9. Check whether the apply uses the saved plan file, and whether the Terraform version is pinned.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "autoResize": true,
      "customData": {
        "node_id": "reading-a-terraform-pipeline"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "rectangle",
      "version": 1,
      "versionNonce": 3581328172,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "node-a8444fec7d9e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": 1320.0,
      "y": 2196.5,
